
LAN definition: group of devices that will receive a braodcast frame.

Vlans (virtual local area network): used when we want to separate devices on the L2. Subnetting separates them on L3. Vlans are configured on the switch in each interfaces, 
but it is not the switch that does the intervlan routing. When configuring Vlans on the switch, if we want a single vlan on an interface, we configure the interface as an
access port. If we want many vlans on a single interface, we configure the interface as a trunk port.

The main trunking protocol is IEEE 802.1Q (dot1Q). When we use vlan, we have to add a tag to the ethernet packet header. The field is called 802.1Q field and is between
the source and the type field. It's a 4 bytes field (32 bits) and it has 4 field inside it, the most important one being the VID field which is where we specify the ID
of the vlan. This VID is 12 bits which mean there are 4096 possible vlan (2^12) (0-4095), but the first and last one are reserved so we can actually have only 4094. 802.1Q
has a feature called native vlan which is vlan 1 by default but can be changed manually. What it does is that all traffic going and coming from the native vlan dont 
have to be tagged, so it is important that the native vlans match between switches if they are manually changed.

**If when configuring an interface on a switch by doing "switchport mode trunk" it gets rejected, we need to do "switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q" first.

Router on a Stick (ROAS): where we divide a single interface to route between multiple vlans ex -> g0/0.10

***Vlan that exist on a switch by default is different than the vlans that are allowed on a trunk by default***

When we configure a router a ROAS, the traffic that is leaving it will travel on the destination vlan, not on the source.

Layer 3 Switches (L3): since they are L3, they can route traffic itself between different VLANs and local subnets without sending that traffic to an router using SVI
(Switch Virtual Interfaces). We can assign IP addresses to these interfaces and we can configure the gateway on the pc to be these ip. In most internal local area
network (LAN) cases, letting a Layer 3 switch handle inter-VLAN routing is better than using a router. These are the 4 conditions to have a working SVI:
1. VLAN must exist on the switch (it will nto create it contrarly to access port if it doesn't exist)
2. Switch must have at least one access port in the VLAN in an up/up state, AND/OR one trunk port that allows the VLAN that is in an up/up state
3. VLAN must not be shutdown
4. SVI itself must not be shutdown
